  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">MARISMAS NACIONALES</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Marismas Nacionales is a complex of meandering rivers, deltas, marshes, freshwater lagoons, estuaries, coastal lagoons, intertidal wetlands and coastal dunes. The largest mangrove area on the Pacific coast of Mexico is located here, and more than 125 commercial fishing companies rely on it. It is a key foraging and resting area for 250,000 migrating ducks and 427,000 shorebirds (28 species). This project will set up conservation agreements on 12,350 acres and management agreements on 2,470 acres of ejido lands, and clear natural channels and reforest mangroves on 2,964 acres in those areas. It will also clear dead trees from an additional 12 miles of channels in another area that is already under protection, thereby rehabilitating natural hydrological patterns on 19,760 acres, which in turn will reduce salinity and restore wetland productivity. The project will also monitor water quality, fisheries and bird populations in a tidal basin.</narrative>
  </description>
